<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peace of Phoenice</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/205-bce">205 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Treaty of Phoenice · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-macedonian-war">First Macedonian War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenice">Phoenice</a> was the treaty that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-macedonian-war">First Macedonian War</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon">Philip V of Macedon</a> in 205 BCE. It was negotiated at Phoenice in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> with the mediation of the Epirotes. The terms were broadly favourable to Philip, who kept most of his gains in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a>, while Rome kept its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> on the Adriatic coast and turned its attention to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>.</p></div>
